---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ToolPanel from '../../components/ToolPanel.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Convert FIT to GPX in your browser"
  description="Convert a Garmin or Wahoo FIT activity to a GPX track with elevation, time, heart rate, cadence, temperature and power. No upload, no account."
  schema={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'FIT to GPX converter',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any (web browser)',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  }}
>
  <h1>Convert FIT to GPX</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    FIT is what the device records. GPX is what maps, route planners and most other software will open. Choose a FIT
    activity and download it as a GPX track.
  </p>

  <ToolPanel tool="fit2gpx" />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What carries over</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>In the GPX</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Position, elevation, time</td><td>Yes, as standard GPX 1.1 track points.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heart rate, cadence, temperature</td><td>Yes, using Garmin's TrackPointExtension, which most software reads.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Power</td><td>Yes, as a <code>power</code> extension element.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Laps, calories, device details, training metrics</td><td>No. GPX has no place for them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Records without a GPS position</td><td>Left out. The tool tells you how many.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Because of that last row, an indoor workout cannot become a GPX file at all: it has no track. The tool says so
      instead of writing an empty file.
    </p>
    <p>
      A FIT file that is damaged will often still convert, since the tool reads up to the last good record. To get a
      fixed FIT file instead, use <a href="/repair-fit-file/">repair</a>. Going the other way is
      <a href="/gpx-to-fit/">GPX to FIT</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
